Done. The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 1 test class — the only one in the project — passing).

## Change

In `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeNewOwner(...)`, a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date now fails validation, which causes `addOwner` to return `400 Bad Request`:

```java
if (owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
    owner.setRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now());
} else if (owner.getRegistrationDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {
    return false;
}
```

I placed the check in `normalizeNewOwner` because that is where the controller already funnels its field-level `400` rejections (blank address, invalid telephone, bad postcode) — `addOwner` maps a `false` return to `HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST`. The check runs only when a date was actually supplied (a missing date still defaults to today) and before `rollToBusinessDay`, so it compares the client-supplied value against the server date as specified. I also updated the method's Javadoc to document the new rejection reason.

The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (posting `registrationDate: "2999-01-01"` and expecting 400) passes.

